  "image_name": "0c889c6eb84f5c8d1412bd5403d5c94f.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how the nuclear envelope disassembles and then reforms during mitosis, linking these changes to spindle microtubules and membrane-remodeling factors. Across the top, a cell progresses from interphase through prophase, prometaphase, metaphase, anaphase, and telophase, showing the nucleus breaking down before chromosome segregation and reappearing around the separated chromosome masses at the end.\n\nThe lower schematics focus on mechanism. During nuclear envelope breakdown, nuclear pore components and lamina-associated proteins are modified by mitotic kinases, weakening connections between chromatin, lamins, and inner nuclear membrane proteins. This allows pores and membrane structure to come apart as centrosome-driven microtubules interact with the envelope, including sites of membrane invagination. The envelope appears to fragment into an ER-like membrane network surrounding the mitotic spindle.\n\nAfter chromosome separation, nuclear envelope reformation begins on chromatin. Specific pore and membrane proteins are recruited back, aided by chromatin-associated factors and the Ran system, while membrane sheets wrap around the chromosomes. The final panels suggest that spindle microtubules must be cleared or severed to seal the reforming nucleus, with factors such as dynein, ESCRT-III/VPS4, and spastin helping close membrane gaps and complete pore-containing daughter nuclei.",
